tel 1.0

base umbrageous
import soundness

info   a compiler plugin which shades packages, moving their declarations beneath a new prefix as they compile
detail
    _Umbrageous_ relocates ("shades") packages at compile time. Given a mapping from a package
    to a new prefix, every declaration of that package, and of its subpackages, is compiled as
    if it had been written beneath the prefix, so that two versions of a library, or a library
    and a copy vendored into another, can live on one classpath without their classes
    colliding. Unlike a bytecode shading tool, it works on the source as it is parsed, so the
    shaded names are what the typer, the TASTy and every later phase see.

    The plugin is enabled by passing its jar to the compiler as `-Xplugin:<path to the
    umbrageous plugin jar>` (the jar's `plugin.properties` names `umbrageous.ShaderPlugin`),
    and each mapping as a plugin option of the form `<package>:<new-prefix>`:

    ```text
    -Xplugin:umbrageous-plugin.jar
    -P:umbrageous:testing:shaded.v1
    ```

    With that mapping, a source declaring `package testing.quux` compiles to
    `shaded.v1.testing.quux`. For each source it rewrites three things:

    - a `package` clause whose name is a mapped package, or within one, is moved beneath the
      prefix; where several mappings match, the longest package wins;
    - every `package` clause gains a wildcard import of each mapping's prefix (except the
      mapping which moved that clause), so `import testing.quux.*` in another package
      still resolves, now to the shaded package;
    - a fully-qualified reference `_root_.testing.…` to a mapped package is redirected to
      `_root_.shaded.v1.testing.…`.

    An option which is not of the form `<package>:<new-prefix>` is reported as a warning and
    ignored. The prefix may contain dots.

    The library is the single JVM-only source set `umbrageous.plugin`, depending on the Scala
    compiler and nothing of Soundness's. It is published as a standalone artifact rather than
    in a Soundness bundle, since a `-Xplugin` jar must declare exactly one plugin. Its two
    classes are exported to the `soundness` umbrella, though no program needs them at run time.

refer umbrageous.ShaderPlugin
refer umbrageous.Shader

entry .ShaderPlugin   the compiler plugin class, named in umbrageous's `plugin.properties`
  detail
      The `StandardPlugin` the compiler instantiates when umbrageous's jar is passed with
      `-Xplugin:`. It installs a single phase, `Shader`, handing it the plugin's options, each
      of which is a mapping `<package>:<new-prefix>` given as `-P:umbrageous:<package>:<new-prefix>`.
      It is not used directly.
  refer umbrageous.Shader
  entry #name   the plugin's name, `umbrageous`, by which its options are addressed

entry .Shader   the plugin phase which moves mapped packages beneath their prefixes, after parsing
  detail
      A phase named `shade`, running after `parser` and before `typer`, over the untyped tree
      of each compilation unit. It is constructed by `ShaderPlugin` with the plugin's options,
      and parses each as a `<package>:<new-prefix>` mapping, warning about and skipping any
      other. It then rewrites each `package` clause of a mapped package beneath its prefix,
      adds a wildcard import of every other mapping's prefix to each `package` clause, and
      redirects each `_root_`-qualified reference into a mapped package. It is not used
      directly.
  refer umbrageous.ShaderPlugin
  entry #phaseName   the phase's name, `shade`
